into wire.he
lang he
use os, crypto.ecc, crypto.hash, net.quic_wire

A standalone program that checks the wire level of QUIC in `net.quic_wire` against the worked examples of RFC 9001 Appendix A and RFC 9000 Appendix A, and prints one line per check. Everything named in this paragraph already exists in the imported library modules: this program must not define any of it, and defines only the constants and helpers in the fenced block below and `main`. All of these are in namespace `net`; every "bytes" result is a growable byte array and every bytes argument a read-only byte slice. `net::qk_initial(connection id, server bool)` gives a `net::QKeys` whose fields `key`, `iv`, `hp` and `sec` are bytes; `net::qk_from(suite int, secret)` gives a QKeys from a traffic secret; `net::qk_update(keys)` gives the QKeys after a key update. `net::qh_long(kind int, destination id, source id, token, number length int, number int, payload length int)` gives the bytes of a long header and `net::qh_short(destination id, phase int, number length int, number int)` those of a short header. `net::qp_seal(keys, header, number length int, number int, payload)` gives the protected packet as bytes. `net::qh_parse(packet, own id length int)` gives a `net::QHdr` with bools `ok` and `lg`, ints `ty`, `pno` and `stop`, and bytes `dcid` and `scid`. `net::qp_open(keys, packet, number offset int, largest number so far int)` gives a `net::QOpen` with a bool `ok`, an int `pn` and bytes `data`. `net::qv_len(value int)` gives the encoded length; `net::qv_put(out, value int)` appends the encoding to a growable byte array; `net::qv_get(bytes, offset int)` gives two ints, the value and the next offset, the value being -1 when the bytes end too soon. `net::qtp_new()` gives a `net::QParams` whose field `v` is a list of 17 ints indexed by parameter number and whose fields `odcid` and `iscid` are bytes; `net::qtp_put(params, server bool)` gives bytes; `net::qtp_get(bytes, server bool)` gives a QParams and a bool. From `crypto`: `crypto::hexb(text)` gives bytes from hex text. The helper `eqh(bytes, hex text)` is true when the bytes equal the hex.

The constants and helpers, exactly as written here. `frames_ok` builds one frame of every kind the library writes, checks that each one alone and all of them in a row read back with the same values, that the other stream frame forms read correctly, and that no cut of the row is read past its end.

```he
A2_FRAME := ""
A2_PACKET := ""
A3_PAYLOAD := "02000000000600405a020000560303eefce7f7b37ba1d1632e96677825ddf73988cfc79825df566dc5430b9a045a1200130100002e00330024001d00209d3c940d89690b84d08a60993c144eca684d1081287c834d5311bcf32bb9da1a002b00020304"
A3_PACKET := "cf000000010008f067a5502a4262b5004075c0d95a482cd0991cd25b0aac406a5816b6394100f37a1c69797554780bb38cc5a99f5ede4cf73c3ec2493a1839b3dbcba3f6ea46c5b7684df3548e7ddeb9c3bf9c73cc3f3bded74b562bfb19fb84022f8ef4cdd93795d77d06edbb7aaf2f58891850abbdca3d20398c276456cbc42158407dd074ee"
eqh(b const byte[:], hex const byte[:]) bool: h := crypto::hexb(hex); b == h[..]
add(fs net::QFrame[>..]&, ty int, a int, b int, c int, fin bool, data const byte[:], tok const byte[:], nr int):
	f ~= net::QFrame{}; f.ty = ty; f.a = a; f.b = b; f.c = c; f.fin = fin; f.data.append(data); f.tok.append(tok)
	for i nr: f.rg.push(i * 37 % 70 + i * 1000)
	fs.push(f)
same(x const net::QFrame&, y const net::QFrame&) bool:
	ok ~= x.ty == y.ty && x.a == y.a && x.b == y.b && x.c == y.c && x.fin == y.fin && x.data[..] == y.data[..] && x.tok[..] == y.tok[..] && x.rg.len == y.rg.len
	if ok:
		for i x.rg.len: ok = ok && x.rg[i] == y.rg[i]
	ok
frames_ok() bool:
	fs net::QFrame[>..] ~= []; big := 4611686018427387903; all byte[>..] ~= []; ok ~= true
	add(fs, 1, 0, 0, 0, false, "", "", 0); add(fs, 2, 1000000, 250, 0, false, "", "", 1); add(fs, 2, big, 16384, 0, false, "", "", 5); add(fs, 4, 4, 77, 1073741824, false, "", "", 0); add(fs, 5, 8, 63, 0, false, "", "", 0)
	add(fs, 6, 16383, 0, 0, false, "hello crypto", "", 0); add(fs, 7, 0, 0, 0, false, "token", "", 0); add(fs, 8, 12, 0, 0, false, "abc", "", 0); add(fs, 8, 3, 4611686018427387000, 0, true, "", "", 0); add(fs, 0, 1, 0, 0, false, "", "", 0)
	add(fs, 16, 64, 0, 0, false, "", "", 0); add(fs, 17, 5, 16384, 0, false, "", "", 0); add(fs, 18, 100, 0, 0, false, "", "", 0); add(fs, 19, 1152921504606846976, 0, 0, false, "", "", 0); add(fs, 20, 1, 0, 0, false, "", "", 0); add(fs, 21, 2, 3, 0, false, "", "", 0)
	add(fs, 22, 7, 0, 0, false, "", "", 0); add(fs, 23, 9, 0, 0, false, "", "", 0); add(fs, 24, 5, 2, 0, false, "12345678", "0123456789abcdef", 0); add(fs, 25, 4, 0, 0, false, "", "", 0); add(fs, 26, 0, 0, 0, false, "pathchal", "", 0); add(fs, 27, 0, 0, 0, false, "pathresp", "", 0)
	add(fs, 28, 376, 6, 0, false, "no application protocol", "", 0); add(fs, 29, 7, 0, 0, false, "bye", "", 0); add(fs, 30, 0, 0, 0, false, "", "", 0)
	for i fs.len:
		one byte[>..] ~= []; net::qf_put(one, fs[i]); net::qf_put(all, fs[i]); g, e := net::qf_get(one[..], 0); ok = ok && e == one.len && same(g, fs[i])
	o ~= 0; k ~= 0
	while o >= 0 && o < all.len && k < fs.len: g, e := net::qf_get(all[..], o); ok = ok && e > o && same(g, fs[k]); o = e; k += 1
	ok = ok && o == all.len && k == fs.len
	s1 byte[>..] ~= [8, 4, 120, 121]; g1, e1 := net::qf_get(s1[..], 0); ok = ok && e1 == 4 && g1.ty == 8 && g1.a == 4 && g1.b == 0 && !g1.fin && g1.data[..] == "xy"
	s2 byte[>..] ~= [13, 4, 16, 122]; g2, e2 := net::qf_get(s2[..], 0); ok = ok && e2 == 4 && g2.ty == 8 && g2.a == 4 && g2.b == 16 && g2.fin && g2.data[..] == "z"
	s3 byte[>..] ~= [3, 9, 1, 0, 2, 5, 6, 7]; g3, e3 := net::qf_get(s3[..], 0); ok = ok && e3 == 8 && g3.ty == 2 && g3.a == 9 && g3.b == 1 && g3.rg.len == 1 && g3.rg[0] == 2
	s4 byte[>..] ~= [31]; g4, e4 := net::qf_get(s4[..], 0); ok = ok && e4 == -1
	for cut all.len:
		p := all[..cut]; q ~= 0; n ~= 0
		while q >= 0 && q < p.len && n < 100: g, e := net::qf_get(p, q); ok = ok && e <= p.len; q = e; n += 1
	ok
```

`main` prints these lines in this order, each a label followed by true or false.
- "Q1 keys ": the connection id is hex 8394c8f03e515708. Its client keys (server false) have key 1f369613dd76d5467730efcbe3b1a22d, iv fa044b2f42a3fd3b46fb255c, hp 9f50449e04a0e810283a1e9933adedd2 and secret c00cf151ca5be075ed0ebfb5c80323c42d6b7db67881289af4008f1f6c357aea; its server keys have key cf3a5331653c364c88f0f379b6067e37, iv 0ac1493ca1905853b0bba03e, hp c206b8d9b9f0f37644430b490eeaa314 and secret 3c199828fd139efd216c155ad844cc81fb82fa8d7446fa7d78be803acdda951b. True when all eight match.
- "Q1 client initial ": the payload is the bytes of `A2_FRAME` followed by zero bytes up to a length of 1162. The header is the long header of kind 0 for that destination id, an empty source id, an empty token, number length 4, number 2 and payload length 1162. True when the header equals hex c300000001088394c8f03e5157080000449e00000002, sealing the payload with the client keys gives exactly `A2_PACKET`, parsing that packet with own id length 0 is ok, long, of kind 0, with that destination id, number offset 18 and stop 1200, and opening it with the client keys at offset 18 with largest number -1 is ok, gives number 2 and gives back the payload.
- "Q1 server initial ": the payload is the bytes of `A3_PAYLOAD`. The header is the long header of kind 0 with an empty destination id, source id hex f067a5502a4262b5, an empty token, number length 2, number 1 and the payload's length. True when the header equals hex c1000000010008f067a5502a4262b50040750001, sealing with the server keys gives exactly `A3_PACKET`, parsing it is ok with number offset 18 and stop 135, and opening it with the server keys at offset 18 with largest number -1 is ok, gives number 1 and gives back the payload.
- "Q1 chacha20 ": the keys come from suite 0x1303 and the secret hex 9ac312a7f877468ebe69422748ad00a15443f18203a07d6060f688f30f21632b. True when the key is c6d98ff3441c3fe1b2182094f69caa2ed4b716b65488960a7a984979fb23e1c8, the iv e0459b3474bdd0e44a41c144, the hp 25a282b9e82f06f21f488917a4fc8f1b73573685608597d0efcb076b0ab7a7a4, the secret of the updated keys is 1223504755036d556342ee9361d253421a826c9ecdf3c7148684b36b714881f9, the short header for an empty destination id, phase 0, number length 3 and number 654360564 equals hex 4200bff4, sealing the one-byte payload 1 with it gives hex 4cfe4189655e5cd55c41f69080575d7999c25a5bfb, and opening that packet at offset 1 with largest number 654360563 is ok, gives number 654360564 and gives back the one byte.
- "Q1 control ": true when opening the bytes of `A2_PACKET` at offset 18 with largest number -1 is not ok with the client keys of the connection id hex 8394c8f03e515709.
- "Q2 varints ": true when all of this holds. Reading hex c2197c5eff14e88c at offset 0 gives 151288809941952652 and next offset 8; hex 9d7f3e7d gives 494878333 and 4; hex 7bbd gives 15293 and 2; hex 25 gives 37 and 1; hex 4025 gives 37 and 2. For each of the values 0, 63, 64, 16383, 16384, 1073741823, 1073741824 and 4611686018427387903, whose lengths are 1, 1, 2, 2, 4, 4, 8 and 8: the encoded length is right, writing it gives that many bytes, reading them back gives the value and that length as the next offset, and, when the length is more than 1, reading the bytes without the last one gives -1.
- "Q2 frames ": the result of `frames_ok`.
- "Q2 parameters ": make parameters with `net::qtp_new`, set entry 1 to 30000, entry 4 to 1048576, entries 5, 6 and 7 to 65536, 65537 and 65538, entries 8 and 9 to 100 and 101, the `iscid` to "abcdefgh" and the `odcid` to "12345678". The server form is what `net::qtp_put` gives with server true, the client form with server false. True when all of this holds: reading the server form as from a server is good and gives the same 17 entries, the same `iscid` and the same `odcid`; reading the client form as from a client is good, gives the same 17 entries and an empty `odcid`; reading the server form as from a client is not good; reading the client form as from a server is not good; reading the server form followed by itself as from a server is not good.

expect:
Q1 keys true
Q1 client initial true
Q1 server initial true
Q1 chacha20 true
Q1 control true
Q2 varints true
Q2 frames true
Q2 parameters true
